en_watermark_on:
Given the prevailing policy mindset and other factors, by 1880, the United States rarely implemented comprehensive measures to curb inflation, even though it occasionally took some easing measures. For instance, during the period 1867-1871, the US tightened its monetary policy to control inflation, but due to slowed economic growth and increased unemployment, fiscal deficits persisted. Additionally, wage and price discipline broke down, leading to further inflation. In August 1872, regulations were imposed on wages and prices; however, the monetary policy remained loose again, resulting in uncontrolled inflation, so by 1873 the wage-price controls had become unsustainable. Coinciding with this was the dramatic increase in international oil prices and soaring grain prices in the US due to poor harvests in Soviet agriculture, intensifying the country’s inflationary trends. Subsequently, major countries worldwide adopted tight monetary policies, precipitating one of the most severe post-war economic downturns. The US then significantly increased its deficit spending to stimulate the economy. Thus, the country found itself in an inescapable dilemma of either worsening inflation or economic depression. However, some American economists noticed that as stagflation deepened in the US, West Germany, Switzerland, and Japan successfully controlled inflation. Their main experience was:

1. Fiscal deficits were not excessively large, although they faced issues such as rising international raw material prices, economic decline, and increased unemployment;
2. The growth of money supply was tightly controlled;
3. Effective wage and price controls were maintained.

Notably, these countries effectively combined these three elements, promoting mutual progress, which starkly contrasted with the US situation. From this, they concluded that an all-encompassing plan combining these three elements was the best approach for curbing inflation, yielding quick and lasting results. Conversely, vacillating between efforts to control inflation only exacerbated existing problems and distorted the economy, eroding public confidence. Moreover, anti-inflation measures should also mitigate the unfairness and distortions caused by inflation, ensuring that any return of the inflationary state would be avoided once anti-inflation actions ceased.
